  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 159 PM MDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nHAZARDOUS WEATHER POSSIBLE THROUGH FRIDAY: \n\nRemnant moisture from a tropical cyclone formerly known as \nTrudy is moving northeastward across eastern Utah and western \nColorado. A plethora of weather hazards are possible with this \nsystem including flooding, flash flooding, strong to severe \nthunderstorms, and snow above 12,000-13,000 feet. As a result, a\nFlood watch is in effect for parts of southeast Utah and \nsouthwest Colorado through 12 PM Friday. \n\nThis afternoon:\n\nAn initial push of convection has developed over the Four \nCorners region and will pose a localized threat for heavy \nrainfall and flash flooding if stronger storms develop, \nespecially if storms begin to train over the same areas. \nIsolated severe storms capable of producing large hail, damaging\nwind, and brief tornadoes cannot be ruled out due to the \npresence of modest instability and strong shear. However, the \nhighest potential for severe weather will remain off to our \nsouthwest in northwest into central Arizona. Stay weather aware \nif you have outdoor activities planned! If you experience any of\nthe hazards noted above, please report them to NWS Grand \nJunction.\n\nTonight - Thursday night: \n\nWith an anomalously moist tropical airmass overhead, widespread\nrainfall takes hold of the forecast area Wednesday night. \nPlease see the Hydrology Section below for more information \nconcerning the threat for flooding from this period of moderate \nto widespread rainfall. \n\nAnother round of strong to severe storms cannot be ruled out on\nThursday afternoon/evening with marginal instability and strong\nwind shear present. However, widespread cloud cover adds a \nlayer of uncertainty to the forecast and may inhibit surface \nheating. If stronger storms develop, isolated damaging wind, \nlarge hail, brief tornadoes are possible. We have low confidence\nin this aspect of the forecast. \n\nFriday: \n\nModels have continued to key in on a dry slot quickly \nevacuating the tropical moisture from the region on Friday \nmorning. Even after it stops raining, the risk for flooding will\nremain elevated on Friday morning as water continues to work \nthrough the system. Do not let your guard down right away! We'll\nhave a better idea of how long flooding may continue to cause \nissues after we get a better sense of how much rainfall is \nreceived on Thursday. \n\n\nLOOKING AHEAD:\n\nChances for light precipitation (valley rain, mountain snow \nabove 10,000 feet) continue through the weekend as a low \npressure system, currently spinning off the California coast, \ntraverses the Intermountain West. Plenty of uncertainty remains \nin how this low will evolve as it moves inland, but ensemble \nguidance tends to show a weakening system by the time it reaches\nour forecast area. Regardless, this system will provide enough \nforcing for scattered to widespread precipitation as it moves \nthrough, alongside a brief reduction in temperatures.",
